1
00:00:00,360 --> 00:00:03,030
Hi, I'm Hozaifa,
an electrical engineer graduate

2
00:00:03,050 --> 00:00:06,610
from the University of Dhaka,
and this is Prohori.

3
00:00:06,920 --> 00:00:08,550
Prohori means sentry in Bengali.

4
00:00:08,570 --> 00:00:13,870
In 2013, Rana Plaza collapsed a day
after cracks were reported in its walls.

5
00:00:14,390 --> 00:00:17,970
Today, crack width is still
judged by eye with a plastic card.

6
00:00:18,570 --> 00:00:21,130
Prohori measures it in
millimetres from a phone photo,

7
00:00:21,150 --> 00:00:25,050
and it refuses to give a number
when the photo can't support one.

8
00:00:26,840 --> 00:00:31,970
Here's how it works. You tape a printed
board next to the crack and take a photo.

9
00:00:32,370 --> 00:00:37,810
OpenCV 5 finds the ChArUco board,
recovers the wall's pose,

10
00:00:37,830 --> 00:00:42,230
and flattens the crack region to a
known scale in pixels per millimetre.

11
00:00:43,360 --> 00:00:48,570
Then the width is read from the grey level
across the crack, not from a mask edge,

12
00:00:48,590 --> 00:00:53,230
because published benchmarks show
masks are rarely right to the pixel.

13
00:00:54,170 --> 00:00:57,770
Before any number is shown, a quality
gate checks. It checks the photo.

14
00:00:58,210 --> 00:01:01,910
Corners found, blur,
tilt, glare, resolution.

15
00:01:02,610 --> 00:01:05,850
The gate is calibrated with
conformal risk control.

16
00:01:06,360 --> 00:01:10,310
On photos it never saw,
about 97 of every 100 green,

17
00:01:10,330 --> 00:01:13,290
certified readings were within tolerance.

18
00:01:14,440 --> 00:01:17,230
If the photo isn't good
enough, it doesn't guess.

19
00:01:18,140 --> 00:01:20,070
That's where the agent comes in.

20
00:01:20,650 --> 00:01:22,170
It never looks at pixels.

21
00:01:22,190 --> 00:01:26,910
It gets the numbers OpenCV measured
and picks one of seven actions:

22
00:01:27,370 --> 00:01:30,770
accept, move closer, hold steadier,

23
00:01:31,050 --> 00:01:34,330
change the light, re-place the board,

24
00:01:34,720 --> 00:01:39,900
accept with a wider uncertainty,
or stop and log it.

25
00:01:41,560 --> 00:01:43,850
Then it tells you what
to do in plain words.

26
00:01:44,940 --> 00:01:46,490
Here the photo was too far.

27
00:01:46,790 --> 00:01:50,950
The agent said, "move closer,"
and the next photo was certified.

28
00:01:51,360 --> 00:01:54,830
We tested it on held-out cases
against a fixed rule list

29
00:01:54,850 --> 00:01:57,810
which we wrote ourselves,
and against random choices.

30
00:01:58,550 --> 00:02:00,670
Every instruction it writes is checked

31
00:02:00,690 --> 00:02:03,530
against the measured
numbers before you see it.

32
00:02:04,250 --> 00:02:06,370
The chart shows how often each one

33
00:02:06,390 --> 00:02:10,490
turns a bad photo into
a certified reading.

34
00:02:11,290 --> 00:02:12,950
Everything runs on AWS.

35
00:02:13,730 --> 00:02:17,770
A Graviton server runs
OpenCV behind HTTPS.

36
00:02:18,380 --> 00:02:21,870
DynamoDB keeps every visit
and every agent decision.

37
00:02:22,250 --> 00:02:26,130
S3 keeps the evidence photos
with faces blurred before saving,

38
00:02:26,170 --> 00:02:29,790
and if a crack widens by more
than the measurement uncertainty,

39
00:02:30,920 --> 00:02:35,390
SNS emails an engineer
who has to approve it.

40
00:02:36,630 --> 00:02:39,890
We also run the whole pipeline
on Graviton4 with COOL,

41
00:02:40,330 --> 00:02:41,930
OpenCV's optimised build.

42
00:02:42,340 --> 00:02:46,530
We check that it gives the
same width as stock OpenCV,

43
00:02:46,600 --> 00:02:49,350
measured speed and cost
per thousand photos,

44
00:02:50,040 --> 00:02:52,070
and found and documented a crash

45
00:02:52,090 --> 00:02:55,090
that only happens in COOL
with more than one thread.

46
00:02:56,160 --> 00:02:57,160
Results.

47
00:02:57,410 --> 00:03:02,030
On a printed panel with known widths,
held-out from all tuning, the average

48
00:03:02,050 --> 00:03:04,530
error is under a tenth of a millimetre,

49
00:03:04,970 --> 00:03:08,490
and lower again on the
cracks the gate certifies.

50
00:03:08,910 --> 00:03:11,390
The certified readings
meet the accuracy bar

51
00:03:11,410 --> 00:03:15,150
reported by recent published
smartphone methods.

52
00:03:15,850 --> 00:03:18,990
Every number and every
failure case is in the report.

53
00:03:19,750 --> 00:03:23,270
Prohori doesn't decide
whether a building is safe.

54
00:03:23,670 --> 00:03:25,070
That's an engineer's job.

55
00:03:25,340 --> 00:03:28,790
It gives them a number, how sure
that number is, how it changed

56
00:03:28,810 --> 00:03:31,950
since the last visit,
and a request to come and look.

57
00:03:32,880 --> 00:03:36,530
The code, the data, and every
result are public. Thank you.
